Implement user story AMSD-1820: [Mozio] - The Promo code amount is NOT displayed as expected for Return trip tickets in the Mozio email confirmation

## Story Description
[Mozio] - The Promo code amount is NOT displayed as expected for Return trip tickets in the Mozio email confirmation

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/clients/mozio/mappers/map-to-sanitized-mozio-dispatch.ts** (`mapToSanitizedMozioDispatch`): This function constructs the dispatch payload (including report.price.discount) that flows into the Mozio email confirmation. It blindly passes through dispatch.report.price.discount only if present on the individual dispatch. For a round-trip order, Mozio returns two dispatches — outbound (isReturn:false) and return (isReturn:true). The promo/discount is typically attached only to the outbound dispatch by Mozio's API, so the return-trip dispatch has no discount field, causing the conditional on line 42 to evaluate to undefined. This is the computation site that decides what discount value the email sees — it is not merely a presentation reader.
  - **Minimal fix:** The discount conditional on lines 42-47 only reads discount from the current single dispatch. For return-trip dispatches (dispatch.isReturn === true), the Mozio API does not attach the promo discount to the return dispatch — it lives on the outbound dispatch. The fix: when dispatch.isReturn is true and dispatch.report.price.discount is absent, the function must still surface the discount. Since mapToSanitizedMozioDispatch currently receives only a single dispatch, the caller (logSubmitOrderPayloadService) should be updated to pass the sibling outbound dispatch's discount for return trips, OR mapToSanitizedMozioDispatch should accept an optional fallback discount parameter. The minimal change: add an optional `fallbackDiscount` param to mapToSanitizedMozioDispatch, and on line 42 change the condition to `dispatch.report.price.discount ?? fallbackDiscount`, so the return-trip dispatch inherits the promo discount from its outbound sibling. The existing maskSensitiveData helper (already imported at line 1) is reused for the discount name.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a return-trip Mozio order with a promo code is placed, the Mozio email confirmation displays the correct promo code discount amount on the return-trip ticket
- When a one-way (non-return) Mozio order with a promo code is placed, the Mozio email confirmation displays the correct promo code discount amount (no regression)
- When a return-trip Mozio order with no promo code is placed, the Mozio email confirmation does not display a promo code discount amount on either leg

